00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:13.410
they had a very small summer school.

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:15.660
They had summer school, they
didn't have a case of COVID,

00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:17.600
they didn't have a positive case.

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:20.530
That, I would say that's luck.

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:22.910
Because when I say Newark did it well,

00:27:22.910 --> 00:27:25.910
it's because if you have a positive case,

00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:27.940
it's a matter of how you deal with it,

00:27:27.940 --> 00:27:29.700
how you create trust

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:32.910
and how you actually do
the work you have to do to,

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:34.830
to shut down the school,

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:37.930
make sure it's communicated
that there's a positive case,

00:27:37.930 --> 00:27:40.030
make sure you make sure that

00:27:40.030 --> 00:27:42.900
that you do the contact tracing,

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:44.720
so that you isolate everybody

00:27:44.720 --> 00:27:48.300
who was in contact with that person.

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:50.850
And Newark did that well, and it created

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:53.350
it created some credibility.

00:27:53.350 --> 00:27:55.080
But think about what's going on.

00:27:55.080 --> 00:27:57.950
We spent all this time
talking about logistics,

00:27:57.950 --> 00:27:59.646
and how little time talking about

00:27:59.646 --> 00:28:01.900
what we need to do in
terms of instruction,

00:28:01.900 --> 00:28:04.973
whether it's hybrid or
whether it's on remote.

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:07.443
- No, absolutely.

00:28:07.443 --> 00:28:08.920
And one last question on the logistics,

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:09.860
because the instruction

00:28:09.860 --> 00:28:12.440
is at least as important in this world.

00:28:12.440 --> 00:28:13.570
Obviously when you read the headlines

00:28:13.570 --> 00:28:14.850
especially with the last few weeks,

00:28:14.850 --> 00:28:17.460
this notion of potential strikes,

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:19.334
have at least been surfaced.

00:28:19.334 --> 00:28:22.140
If I'm, based on our conversation so far,

00:28:22.140 --> 00:28:24.520
I'm perceiving that what you're saying is,

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:27.880
strikes where there are not precautions,

00:28:27.880 --> 00:28:29.580
where community spread is so high,

00:28:29.580 --> 00:28:32.390
that it would be just
fundamentally unsafe.

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:33.553
to send someone into that.
- Correct.

00:28:33.553 --> 00:28:35.930
- Is that what y'all are advocating?

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:36.931
- Correct.
- How y'all thinking

00:28:36.931 --> 00:28:37.790
about that?

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:41.483
- Right, the word in front of
strike, was the word safety.

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:46.730
Safety, strikes and labor have
a very particular meaning.

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:50.440
And they are only a last
resort if you can't do,

00:28:50.440 --> 00:28:53.500
if you can't make it
safe in any other way.

00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:58.340
We feel that we are now the
conscience of the country,

00:28:58.340 --> 00:29:02.670
of making sure our kids, our communities

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:04.950
and our educators are safe.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:07.950
And the fact that others are reckless,

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:11.040
makes us need to be more responsible.

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:14.670
Like no one got into teaching
to be a safety monitor.

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:19.190
And I'm a social studies
teacher and a lawyer.

00:29:19.190 --> 00:29:22.940
So learning all this about,
learning all of the science,

00:29:22.940 --> 00:29:24.770
was hard for me to learn.

00:29:24.770 --> 00:29:28.010
But you have to learn it
in the middle of a pandemic

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:30.240
and you have to have those guardrails.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:33.210
And I have to look at a lot
of people straight in the eyes

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:37.510
and say that in order to advise

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:41.170
that a school building should reopen,

00:29:41.170 --> 00:29:44.570
that the kind of things that are done

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:46.950
to prevent virus spread are in there.

00:29:46.950 --> 00:29:50.070
And if they're not in
there, I in good conscience,

00:29:50.070 --> 00:29:53.680
cannot advise that a
building should reopen.

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:54.513
It doesn't mean

00:29:54.513 --> 00:29:56.860
that there's not gonna
be a COVID positive case.

00:29:56.860 --> 00:30:00.800
But we need to make sure, that
everything we can do is done.

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:02.300
That's what hospitals do.

00:30:02.300 --> 00:30:04.380
That's what nursing home should have done.

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:06.370
And we need to do that
for schools as well.

00:30:06.370 --> 00:30:08.460
We know what we need to do.

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:11.400
Those and yes, it's a
logistical challenge,

00:30:11.400 --> 00:30:15.860
but if others refuse to do it,
that's why you have unions.

00:30:15.860 --> 00:30:18.110
That's why you have collective bargaining.

00:30:18.110 --> 00:30:20.330
That's why you have collective Goodwill.

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:23.756
Sometimes you have to take
the more responsibility

00:30:23.756 --> 00:30:26.373
to make sure that things
are safe for people,

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:29.390
- Right, it is, well and to kind of

00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:31.120
the most fundamental purpose of unions,

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:32.720
which is workplace safety.
- Right.

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:35.810
- Which, and this isn't
some blanket statement that

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:38.010
no school openings at all.

00:30:38.010 --> 00:30:38.843
- It's only when,
- No.

00:30:38.843 --> 00:30:41.459
- it's unsafe.
- Exactly.

00:30:41.459 --> 00:30:45.051
And frankly that's why even
at our convention last week,

00:30:45.051 --> 00:30:47.380
somebody raised that motion.

00:30:47.380 --> 00:30:50.260
We had a virtual convention,
we had resolutions,

00:30:50.260 --> 00:30:53.010
and there were two or three resolutions

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:55.827
where people tagged on a motion that says,

00:30:55.827 --> 00:30:59.117
"We will not reopen schools
unless the virus rate is zero,

00:30:59.117 --> 00:31:00.960
"or unless there is a vaccine."

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:04.160
And those motions were
rejected by my membership.

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:09.160
We know that there is not
a zero risk situation here.

00:31:09.332 --> 00:31:13.380
We know that that kids
need in-school schooling.

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:15.400
Teachers would tell you that,

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:17.307
pre-pandemic they would have said,

00:31:17.307 --> 00:31:18.577
"We need to see our kids,

00:31:18.577 --> 00:31:20.527
"we need to have smaller class sizes

00:31:20.527 --> 00:31:23.207
"and remote instruction
doesn't work very well,

00:31:23.207 --> 00:31:25.210
"Sal Khan notwithstanding."

00:31:25.210 --> 00:31:26.705
That's what people would have said.

00:31:26.705 --> 00:31:29.300
Now they'll say it even more.

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:31.030
But it has to be safe.

00:31:31.030 --> 00:31:34.540
Because if it's not safe,
then what ends up happening,

00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:36.380
is a school outbreak,

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:39.240
could end up reinfecting
the entire community.

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:43.100
And so places like New York
and New Jersey and Connecticut,

00:31:43.100 --> 00:31:45.210
that went through those sacrifices,

00:31:45.210 --> 00:31:48.690
that the businesses that
didn't reopen early,

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:51.690
why would we put them through this again,

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:53.723
after all the work that they have done?

00:31:53.723 --> 00:31:57.916
We have to educate our kids,
but we have to make it safe.

00:31:57.916 --> 00:31:58.960
- No, that makes sense.

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:00.190
I mean my wife and I
were talking about it.

00:32:00.190 --> 00:32:01.023
She's a physician

00:32:01.023 --> 00:32:03.270
and even she works for
the County hospital.

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:05.810
She's a rheumatologist, so
she's not a pulmonologist.

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:07.690
She's not one of the
frontline workers here.

00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:09.870
But for the rheumatologists, they are two.

00:32:09.870 --> 00:32:11.831
They're making sure that
they have all the PPE,

00:32:11.831 --> 00:32:14.100
patients that they can
see remotely they are,

00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:16.370
the patients that they
absolutely have to see,

00:32:16.370 --> 00:32:18.630
the precautions are
incredible that they're taking

00:32:18.630 --> 00:32:20.480
with the monitoring,
the temperature checks,

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:21.660
the PPE, et cetera.

00:32:21.660 --> 00:32:24.940
And then if you didn't have those,

00:32:24.940 --> 00:32:26.760
my wife did not have those precautions

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:28.160
as a healthcare professional,

00:32:28.160 --> 00:32:33.160
I would not encourage her to to show up.

00:32:33.537 --> 00:32:35.500
My mother-in-law lives with us.

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:36.800
I mean it's just
- Yeah.

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:38.260
- for the safety of the community.

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:40.090
I mean going into the instructional side,

00:32:40.090 --> 00:32:42.670
which as we've both been alluding to,

00:32:42.670 --> 00:32:44.760
we've been so focused on
logistics, the whole world has

00:32:44.760 --> 00:32:49.130
that the teacher training
the PD days, et cetera,

00:32:49.130 --> 00:32:50.220
there's a lot of questions coming in.

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:52.709
I'll try to give a
synopsis of some of them.

00:32:52.709 --> 00:32:55.350
This is a question from
a teacher on Facebook.

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:58.360
Randi will remote learning
make teachers obsolete,

00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:00.190
will it damage the profession?

00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:03.690
And then from Facebook,
Sara Jobin is asking,

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:05.360
what kind of student engagement

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:07.160
through remote learning
is actually working?

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:08.484
So what does good distance learning

00:33:08.484 --> 00:33:09.317
look like?
- Yeah.

00:33:09.317 --> 00:33:11.070
- Even you were very kind
when you said Khan Academy.

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:12.397
But I think it's working,

00:33:12.397 --> 00:33:14.270
even Khan Academy in certain places,

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:16.410
I think some places it's
not working optimally

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:18.410
because they're not
getting enough supports,

00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:19.680
the teachers aren't
getting enough support.

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:21.622
So I'd love your frame on

00:33:21.622 --> 00:33:23.930
what should that professional development

00:33:23.930 --> 00:33:25.520
look like for teachers?
- Right.

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:29.263
- What does at least decent
distance learning look like?

00:33:30.170 --> 00:33:33.880
- So all kids are different

00:33:33.880 --> 00:33:35.230
and all ages are different.

00:33:35.230 --> 00:33:38.250
So again, there's not a one size fits all

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:39.590
in terms of distance learning.

00:33:39.590 --> 00:33:43.130
But for the teacher who
raised that question,

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:45.795
I think that this, that we have now,

00:33:45.795 --> 00:33:48.486
that the pandemic has now proven

00:33:48.486 --> 00:33:52.460
that distance learning is a
supplement not a substitute

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:55.020
for in-person teaching and learning.

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:58.470
That the alchemy that happens in a class,

00:33:58.470 --> 00:34:00.716
the engagement in-person

00:34:00.716 --> 00:34:03.510
is really, really important for kids.

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:07.330
But it can be a supplement,
and ultimately right now,

00:34:07.330 --> 00:34:09.780
I see this year as a bridge year.

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:11.400
We're in the middle of a pandemic.

00:34:11.400 --> 00:34:13.593
Just like with TB, just
like what's the Spanish flu

00:34:13.593 --> 00:34:17.110
of a hundred years ago,
just like with polio,

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:18.950
they have to take these things seriously.

00:34:18.950 --> 00:34:22.510
And then we have to catch,
we have to help kids

00:34:22.510 --> 00:34:26.760
feel confident that they will
be able to achieve regardless.

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:30.760
And then as these things
subside, or we get a vaccine,

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:33.030
or we get therapeutics like in AIDS,

00:34:33.030 --> 00:34:35.810
we don't have a vaccine and
AIDS, but we have therapeutics.

00:34:35.810 --> 00:34:38.189
We're able, you can see what has happened

00:34:38.189 --> 00:34:42.350
in terms of the LGBTQ community,

00:34:42.350 --> 00:34:45.350
in terms of what happened in the aftermath

00:34:45.350 --> 00:34:48.959
of having the therapeutics,

00:34:48.959 --> 00:34:52.630
in the late 80s early 90s.

00:34:52.630 --> 00:34:55.880
What I think we have to do
in terms of instruction,

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:57.690
this is what I've seen work.

00:34:57.690 --> 00:35:00.392
And frankly, it's just
from talking to people

00:35:00.392 --> 00:35:05.060
who have done it over the course
of the last several months.

00:35:05.060 --> 00:35:07.889
Number one, we have to be mindful

00:35:07.889 --> 00:35:11.750
of how much screen time people have.

00:35:11.750 --> 00:35:13.160
We also have to make sure

00:35:13.160 --> 00:35:14.925
that there's real group instruction.

00:35:14.925 --> 00:35:19.925
Asymmetric learning can
actually really work,

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:23.460
but schedules are really, really
important for younger kids.

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:27.220
And when I say asymptomatic
learning can work,

00:35:27.220 --> 00:35:29.490
high school teachers have said to me,

00:35:29.490 --> 00:35:31.730
that they could actually tape a lesson

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:35.470
and then use screen time
to reinforce that lesson.

00:35:35.470 --> 00:35:40.470
You can use, I'm a big believer
in getting some tents up

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:43.900
and having some open air classrooms

00:35:43.900 --> 00:35:46.130
in parks in September and October

00:35:46.130 --> 00:35:48.860
and doing small group
instruction that way,

00:35:48.860 --> 00:35:49.940
just like some

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:54.840
parents in well-off communities

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:57.000
are thinking about hiring a teacher

00:35:57.000 --> 00:35:58.950
and doing a pod in that way.

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:03.950
But for younger kids, direct
instruction like this may work,

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:08.780
but you cannot be on a screen
for more than 45 minutes

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:10.076
at any particular time.

00:36:10.076 --> 00:36:11.962
Kids have to be off the screen,

00:36:11.962 --> 00:36:14.200
they have to be able to engage

00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:16.550
in their own kind of learning,

00:36:16.550 --> 00:36:19.420
and we have to figure out
how we get kids together

00:36:19.420 --> 00:36:21.220
in some way, form or fashion

00:36:21.220 --> 00:36:23.230
to stop the social isolation.

00:36:23.230 --> 00:36:26.290
In terms of older kids,
I think you flip it.

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:29.680
You can actually do direct instruction

00:36:29.680 --> 00:36:31.790
asynchronisticly if you want to,

00:36:31.790 --> 00:36:35.040
you can just like with college kids,

00:36:35.040 --> 00:36:36.750
you can tape a lecture.

00:36:36.750 --> 00:36:39.820
But then you really need the group work

00:36:39.820 --> 00:36:42.900
to reinforce or to create some alchemy.

00:36:42.900 --> 00:36:47.130
And that's, but just like you
have done so much work Sal,

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.540
in this, we have Share My Lesson,

00:36:49.540 --> 00:36:51.585
we have unprecedented numbers of people

00:36:51.585 --> 00:36:53.300
who have joined Share My Lesson.

00:36:53.300 --> 00:36:56.360
We make it for free for
everyone, for parents,

00:36:56.360 --> 00:36:58.960
for kids, for teachers.

00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:02.342
And so, and every day we
put tricks of the trade up.

00:37:02.342 --> 00:37:04.680
The other thing I think is,

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:07.713
you could do things like using TV,

00:37:09.800 --> 00:37:14.380
Fox in Washington DC,

00:37:14.380 --> 00:37:15.846
would have like an hour

00:37:15.846 --> 00:37:18.943
where it was dedicated every morning to,

00:37:20.830 --> 00:37:24.150
to a different lesson by a
different master teacher.

00:37:24.150 --> 00:37:25.550
You saw that in

00:37:27.610 --> 00:37:30.220
California as well in the spring.

00:37:30.220 --> 00:37:34.830
I think that lectures from the masters,

00:37:34.830 --> 00:37:38.120
finding ways to have

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:40.200
really interesting content,

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:42.800
is gonna also be really important.

00:37:42.800 --> 00:37:46.140
Doing things in a way where
families can come together.

00:37:46.140 --> 00:37:49.740
I love the fact that we had
Lin-Manuel at our convention.

00:37:49.740 --> 00:37:54.513
I love that Disney decided to put that on,

00:37:55.540 --> 00:37:58.260
on air around July 4th weekend.

00:37:58.260 --> 00:38:01.660
Having a family watch "Hamilton,"

00:38:01.660 --> 00:38:03.763
and then you doing some kind of,

00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:08.090
group instruction follow-up,

00:38:08.090 --> 00:38:09.760
and you could do it with the whole family.

00:38:09.760 --> 00:38:12.250
You make it a family enterprise

00:38:12.250 --> 00:38:15.440
as opposed to kids being isolated.

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:18.210
Or you can do what some people,

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:19.850
some families have done this summer.

00:38:19.850 --> 00:38:23.120
Which is they have a pod in the backyard,

00:38:23.120 --> 00:38:26.400
or you could do it in the middle of,

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:29.570
within a housing project,
in the middle of the lawn,

00:38:29.570 --> 00:38:31.240
in the middle of the housing project.

00:38:31.240 --> 00:38:36.240
So we need to really kinda
think about different ways

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:39.980
of getting together,
having that engagement,

00:38:39.980 --> 00:38:42.080
using the screen for certain things

00:38:42.080 --> 00:38:44.233
and reinforcement for other things,

00:38:45.550 --> 00:38:48.223
dependent upon pedagogically
where kids are.

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:50.910
- That makes a ton of sense

00:38:50.910 --> 00:38:52.810
and rhymes or in some ways

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:55.697
it's exactly what I think what
we've been trying to advocate

00:38:55.697 --> 00:38:57.480
very, very similar things.

00:38:57.480 --> 00:38:59.450
You can have asynchronous learning,

00:38:59.450 --> 00:39:00.860
obviously tools like Khan Academy.

00:39:00.860 --> 00:39:03.790
There's other tools out there
where kids can get practice,

00:39:03.790 --> 00:39:05.700
feedback, teachers can monitor.

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:09.960
But then the necessity of
having some form of whole group

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:13.590
synchronous community
building, I loved your ideas,

00:39:13.590 --> 00:39:14.890
especially if you could figure out

00:39:14.890 --> 00:39:16.290
ways that you could do it in-person,

00:39:16.290 --> 00:39:18.570
somehow at a park and smaller groups,

00:39:18.570 --> 00:39:20.420
but at least have some time.

00:39:20.420 --> 00:39:21.970
But what I was hearing is 45,

00:39:21.970 --> 00:39:25.030
you don't wanna stretch of more
than 45 minutes on a screen.

00:39:25.030 --> 00:39:26.750
It's just bad for the eyes and the brain.

00:39:26.750 --> 00:39:27.583
I think that's true

00:39:27.583 --> 00:39:31.032
for adults as well.
- Yeah, I agree.

00:39:31.032 --> 00:39:33.770
- But obviously just, make
sure there's breaks in there,

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:36.010
but kids feel like they're
part of a community.

00:39:36.010 --> 00:39:38.800
Is there some baseline recommendation?

00:39:38.800 --> 00:39:42.610
I've seen, in the spring we
saw versions was just like,

00:39:42.610 --> 00:39:45.380
here's a couple of links
to some virtual schools

00:39:45.380 --> 00:39:47.380
that are doing everything asynchronously,

00:39:47.380 --> 00:39:49.020
which doesn't seem like a great experience

00:39:49.020 --> 00:39:50.880
based on what you just described.

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:52.470
All the way to, I've seen some schools

00:39:52.470 --> 00:39:54.640
that have been doing
everything you just described.

00:39:54.640 --> 00:39:57.940
What's kind of a, a
minimum viable experience

00:39:57.940 --> 00:39:58.773
in your mind
(laughing)

00:39:58.773 --> 00:40:01.853
that at least parents and
teachers should strive for?

00:40:03.430 --> 00:40:08.430
- So at the end of the
last year, we thought,

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:14.540
we were very big believers
in capstone projects

00:40:14.890 --> 00:40:19.140
done in a developmentally appropriate way.

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:23.180
I mean you're not gonna
have a fourth grader

00:40:23.180 --> 00:40:24.402
write a PhD project

00:40:24.402 --> 00:40:28.680
on Lincoln and the Gettysburg Address.

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:32.003
And why did that not actually,

00:40:32.003 --> 00:40:35.270
eliminate, eradicate racism in America?

00:40:35.270 --> 00:40:37.320
That's not gonna be what
a fourth grader does,

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:39.230
but it could be what a 12th grader does

00:40:39.230 --> 00:40:40.740
and 11th grader does.

00:40:40.740 --> 00:40:43.810
And we've seen in terms
of portfolio schools,

00:40:43.810 --> 00:40:46.300
how they were actually very adept,

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:49.160
at being able to move to remote

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:52.370
because of the kind of
guidance they had done

00:40:52.370 --> 00:40:54.240
in terms of critical thinking

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:56.260
and group work and things like that.

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:59.110
So at the end of last year we thought

00:40:59.110 --> 00:41:03.880
capping off, capping off
what people had done,

00:41:03.880 --> 00:41:06.400
kids had done during the
year was really important

00:41:06.400 --> 00:41:09.308
and we call the capstone projects.

00:41:09.308 --> 00:41:12.250
In terms of the beginning of the year,

00:41:12.250 --> 00:41:16.810
I would actually say as a bottom line,

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:19.391
wellbeing and community building,

00:41:19.391 --> 00:41:22.780
and that the children will be okay,

00:41:22.780 --> 00:41:24.453
it's gonna be most important.

00:41:25.990 --> 00:41:27.283
Pam Cantor,

00:41:30.061 --> 00:41:32.220
who leads turnaround schools,

00:41:32.220 --> 00:41:36.540
the turnaround for children,
said something to us

00:41:36.540 --> 00:41:41.540
at a town hall we did with
the PTA in March or April.

00:41:41.690 --> 00:41:42.973
And she said,

00:41:44.850 --> 00:41:46.790
a calm mind

00:41:46.790 --> 00:41:48.960
will be a learning mind.

00:41:48.960 --> 00:41:53.960
We have to make sure we reach
kids and we create confidence

00:41:55.950 --> 00:41:59.380
and calmness that there'll be okay.

00:41:59.380 --> 00:42:01.530
And that means that the wellbeing issues

00:42:01.530 --> 00:42:03.660
become really important

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:06.710
and the engagement issues
become very important

00:42:06.710 --> 00:42:10.303
as a pathway to getting to instruction.

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:13.623
- I wanna doubly underline that last part.

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:16.810
And people might even be
surprised hearing it from me,

00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:19.327
'cause Khan Academy is
very much focused on,

00:42:19.327 --> 00:42:21.247
"Dude, have you learned
this math or the science

00:42:21.247 --> 00:42:22.880
"or this humanity standard?"

00:42:22.880 --> 00:42:24.200
But I couldn't agree with you more

00:42:24.200 --> 00:42:25.530
on what you just said that,

00:42:25.530 --> 00:42:28.137
so many people right
now are so focused on,

00:42:28.137 --> 00:42:29.327
"Oh, what if my kids don't learn

00:42:29.327 --> 00:42:31.270
"to factor polynomial on time?"

00:42:31.270 --> 00:42:32.810
Well yeah, that is an issue.

00:42:32.810 --> 00:42:35.730
But if their mental health,
if their friendships,

00:42:35.730 --> 00:42:37.996
if their connectedness,
if their confidence

00:42:37.996 --> 00:42:41.380
somehow atrophies or dies
through this process,

00:42:41.380 --> 00:42:42.520
then we have a much

00:42:42.520 --> 00:42:44.640
bigger problem.
- Yup, yup.

00:42:44.640 --> 00:42:46.630
- And how do you address that?

00:42:46.630 --> 00:42:47.980
If we have to be distanced,

00:42:47.980 --> 00:42:49.860
if we can't figure out the
logistics of meeting in a park.

00:42:49.860 --> 00:42:53.830
Is it, you need at
least 30 minutes on Zoom

00:42:53.830 --> 00:42:55.900
together with your friends and teachers.

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:58.360
What does that minimum
situation look like?

00:42:58.360 --> 00:42:59.760
There's another question

00:42:59.760 --> 00:43:01.890
from YouTube Susanna Garcia Dominguez,

00:43:01.890 --> 00:43:02.770
do you think students have to have

00:43:02.770 --> 00:43:05.050
the same number of hours
in online learning?

00:43:05.050 --> 00:43:06.091
Is it nearly six hours?
- No.

00:43:06.091 --> 00:43:08.117
- What is that,
- No,

00:43:08.117 --> 00:43:09.970
I think that some of this,

00:43:09.970 --> 00:43:11.390
I think that some of this,

00:43:11.390 --> 00:43:12.683
we're gonna learn.

00:43:13.520 --> 00:43:16.610
We're trying to create this box.

00:43:16.610 --> 00:43:19.290
Like, okay we have to figure out 180 days.

00:43:19.290 --> 00:43:21.420
Okay, we have to figure
out X number of hours.

00:43:21.420 --> 00:43:24.090
Okay, if we don't do this
what is gonna happen?

00:43:24.090 --> 00:43:26.093
We're in the middle of a pandemic!

00:43:27.220 --> 00:43:29.210
Can we actually, if we could try.

00:43:29.210 --> 00:43:33.400
So yes, I know I'm basically saying

00:43:33.400 --> 00:43:35.740
the rules have to be rewritten.

00:43:35.740 --> 00:43:38.220
The rules have to be rewritten right now.

00:43:38.220 --> 00:43:40.260
The pandemic isn't over.

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:43.100
When it's over, we can actually figure out

00:43:43.100 --> 00:43:45.990
what we need to do moving forward.

00:43:45.990 --> 00:43:50.570
But just like you said, if
we don't actually create

00:43:50.570 --> 00:43:53.380
confidence and engagement for kids,

00:43:53.380 --> 00:43:55.460
if kids are socially isolated,

00:43:55.460 --> 00:43:58.330
why are the pediatricians,
including my sister,

00:43:58.330 --> 00:44:00.050
who is an intensive care pediatrician

00:44:00.050 --> 00:44:01.590
at Montefiore hospital,

00:44:01.590 --> 00:44:06.147
why are they constantly saying,
"The kids, look at the kids,

00:44:06.147 --> 00:44:07.557
"Look at the social isolation,

00:44:07.557 --> 00:44:11.067
"look at the fact that kids
who are not on digital,

00:44:11.067 --> 00:44:13.436
"look at kids that need grab-and-go's."

00:44:13.436 --> 00:44:17.000
Maslow comes before Bloom

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:19.160
any day of the week.

00:44:19.160 --> 00:44:23.870
And we have to make sure that
we meet kids primal needs.

00:44:23.870 --> 00:44:26.590
That has to be our job.

00:44:26.590 --> 00:44:29.370
And if we do and we create confidence,

00:44:29.370 --> 00:44:32.880
we're gonna get to the
other instructional pieces.

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:36.744
And so that's why, I know
all these states today

00:44:36.744 --> 00:44:39.200
what are we gonna do with all our rules?.

00:44:39.200 --> 00:44:44.020
I am focused on how do
we help start school

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:47.550
in a meaningful way, for
kids and for their teachers

00:44:47.550 --> 00:44:50.120
who are in some ways their lifeline

00:44:50.120 --> 00:44:53.340
to the next, to their dreams.

00:44:53.340 --> 00:44:56.700
So is there a minimum?

00:44:56.700 --> 00:44:58.490
I don't know what the minimum.

00:44:58.490 --> 00:45:02.060
We're just, we have a
lot of practices now.

00:45:02.060 --> 00:45:04.830
I don't think we can say
we know what's best yet,

00:45:04.830 --> 00:45:06.670
because they haven't been tested.

00:45:06.670 --> 00:45:11.360
What we do know is that
just like with adults,

00:45:11.360 --> 00:45:16.360
if kids end up having like a
Zoom dinner with their friends,

00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:19.600
just like, my friends and I, we have,

00:45:19.600 --> 00:45:21.990
sometimes we'll have a Zoom drink.

00:45:21.990 --> 00:45:25.890
Or in the midst of the New York shutdowns,

00:45:25.890 --> 00:45:28.060
we'd have a Zoom drink together.

00:45:28.060 --> 00:45:29.710
We'd have a Zoom dinner together.

00:45:29.710 --> 00:45:32.030
We'd get together in that kind of way.

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:34.120
Not more than 45 minutes or an hour

00:45:34.120 --> 00:45:36.870
because of the same reasons.

00:45:36.870 --> 00:45:41.870
But I do think we have to find
a way to get kids together.

00:45:42.574 --> 00:45:46.190
And that creating these kinds of pods

00:45:46.190 --> 00:45:47.410
that families are doing,

00:45:47.410 --> 00:45:51.240
creating ways of getting kids together

00:45:51.240 --> 00:45:55.810
in a socially appropriate
way, wearing masks,

00:45:55.810 --> 00:45:59.580
being physically distant
is very, very important.

00:45:59.580 --> 00:46:02.150
And if we can do that
in terms of schooling,

00:46:02.150 --> 00:46:05.190
if we can use even a school building,

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:07.110
even if we're doing remote

00:46:07.110 --> 00:46:11.270
and we have like a Saturday
afternoon walk with our kids,

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:13.890
that will be important.

00:46:13.890 --> 00:46:16.630
But that's gonna, we're
gonna learn more and more,

00:46:16.630 --> 00:46:21.310
but the point is, it's important
for people to get together,

00:46:21.310 --> 00:46:22.970
and to see each other

00:46:22.970 --> 00:46:25.773
and to be in community with each other.

00:46:27.110 --> 00:46:28.858
- No couldn't agree more.

00:46:28.858 --> 00:46:30.720
And the time has gone by
way faster than I expected.

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:33.070
I could talk to you for
another several hours.

00:46:33.070 --> 00:46:36.080
There's so much, I
wanna learn from you on.

00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:39.970
One kind of last question,
maybe to sum up everything.

00:46:39.970 --> 00:46:42.921
- And I learnt, look, I've
been learning from teachers

00:46:42.921 --> 00:46:47.560
and educator and
paraprofessionals and bus drivers

00:46:47.560 --> 00:46:49.350
and guidance counselors.

00:46:49.350 --> 00:46:54.350
We've basically really taken
in all the learning from people

00:46:54.430 --> 00:46:56.690
all throughout the last several months

00:46:56.690 --> 00:46:59.720
in terms of what has
worked and what hasn't.

00:46:59.720 --> 00:47:00.553
- No, absolutely.

00:47:00.553 --> 00:47:01.570
I mean based on that,

00:47:01.570 --> 00:47:03.840
based on all of these
data points you've seen,

00:47:03.840 --> 00:47:06.160
if we're talking a year from now,

00:47:06.160 --> 00:47:08.620
I'd love to know kind of
both of your hopes and fears.

00:47:08.620 --> 00:47:10.697
The fears, what's kind of the,

00:47:11.550 --> 00:47:15.520
this is clearly suboptimal
situation, on so many dimensions.

00:47:15.520 --> 00:47:16.373
What's some of the things

00:47:16.373 --> 00:47:18.890
that we're gonna have to
repair a year from now

00:47:18.890 --> 00:47:21.170
and are there some silver linings?

00:47:21.170 --> 00:47:23.280
Are there some things that
we might a year from now

00:47:23.280 --> 00:47:25.543
come out a better for?

00:47:26.880 --> 00:47:29.380
- So I'm an optimist.

00:47:29.380 --> 00:47:32.240
So I always have to think
about a silver lining,

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:35.003
even in the midst of these three crises.

00:47:35.930 --> 00:47:40.930
Look, so number one, we
have to fight the pandemic.

00:47:43.120 --> 00:47:46.623
Truth is very important,
science is very important.

00:47:48.580 --> 00:47:52.050
And those things, we need leadership

00:47:52.050 --> 00:47:55.280
that will fight the
pandemic, that's number one.

00:47:55.280 --> 00:47:56.798
And I think frankly,

00:47:56.798 --> 00:48:00.400
even though the country is
very polarized right now,

00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:02.520
more and more and more people see that,

00:48:02.520 --> 00:48:04.570
and that is really important.

00:48:04.570 --> 00:48:08.610
Number two, I think that out of this

00:48:08.610 --> 00:48:12.640
will be a sense that public
schooling is really important

00:48:12.640 --> 00:48:16.270
and the community building and people,

00:48:16.270 --> 00:48:20.520
kids being together
in-person is very important.

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:25.520
And it's not simply the means of parents

00:48:25.692 --> 00:48:26.733
who now

00:48:27.950 --> 00:48:30.130
have a healthy respect

00:48:30.130 --> 00:48:32.810
for that teaching

00:48:32.810 --> 00:48:35.950
is both a science and
an art form and a craft

00:48:35.950 --> 00:48:40.260
as well as a skill, but
that the whole alchemy

00:48:40.260 --> 00:48:42.630
of having public schools is important.

00:48:42.630 --> 00:48:45.450
And that remote can be a supplement,

00:48:45.450 --> 00:48:47.470
but it's not a substitute.

00:48:47.470 --> 00:48:51.060
Number three, I think that this gives

00:48:51.060 --> 00:48:56.060
a new kind of movement builder to science,

00:48:56.720 --> 00:48:58.610
and the importance of science.

00:48:58.610 --> 00:49:03.610
And the importance of data
and understanding the world.

00:49:04.840 --> 00:49:08.860
And understanding what
science means to the world.

00:49:08.860 --> 00:49:13.860
Number four, I think it gives a sense

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:17.280
that we have to work on things equitably.

00:49:17.280 --> 00:49:22.280
That in this pandemic,
just like so much else,

00:49:22.860 --> 00:49:25.653
the people who have gotten
really, really hurt,

00:49:26.710 --> 00:49:31.423
are the people who are most
at risk in normal situations.

00:49:32.980 --> 00:49:34.970
And you see it in terms of

00:49:34.970 --> 00:49:38.410
who's gotten the racial
health disparities.

00:49:38.410 --> 00:49:40.930
You see how racism is really built

00:49:40.930 --> 00:49:44.410
into so many of our structures in America.

00:49:44.410 --> 00:49:49.410
And number five, I would
say that working families

00:49:49.490 --> 00:49:52.910
and working people, are the
ones who have gotten us through

00:49:52.910 --> 00:49:54.510
where we've gotten thus far.

00:49:54.510 --> 00:49:59.510
The grocery workers, the nurses,
the doctors, the EMT folk.

00:50:00.840 --> 00:50:03.110
The people who have
been on the front line,

00:50:03.110 --> 00:50:05.000
actually making sure

00:50:05.000 --> 00:50:08.370
that the rest of us got fed, got helped.

00:50:08.370 --> 00:50:11.280
The teachers who made sure that
kids were engaged last year

00:50:11.280 --> 00:50:13.590
or are trying to figure out right now.

00:50:13.590 --> 00:50:17.840
And so this has not been as important as

00:50:19.560 --> 00:50:21.773
a President is supposed to be,

00:50:25.198 --> 00:50:28.560
or a big media star is supposed to be.

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:32.590
This has been everyday
people and governors

00:50:32.590 --> 00:50:36.480
in many states, who have
really gotten us through this.

00:50:36.480 --> 00:50:39.130
So those are kind of
silver linings that I see.

00:50:39.130 --> 00:50:42.800
I think if we can create a confidence,

00:50:42.800 --> 00:50:44.186
that dreams will not be,

00:50:44.186 --> 00:50:47.234
dreams may be deferred
for a minute or two,

00:50:47.234 --> 00:50:51.880
but we can get the economy back
after the virus is tackled.

00:50:51.880 --> 00:50:56.180
I think if we, if we get
a sense of confidence

00:50:56.180 --> 00:50:57.930
that we can fight the virus,

00:50:57.930 --> 00:51:02.200
and confidence that America
can pull itself up again,

00:51:02.200 --> 00:51:07.200
like it did in the recession,
the Great Depression,

00:51:07.270 --> 00:51:11.950
then I think we will be able
to meet the needs of our kids.

00:51:11.950 --> 00:51:13.806
But if we don't fight the virus

00:51:13.806 --> 00:51:16.550
and if we don't make sure people are safe,

00:51:16.550 --> 00:51:19.793
then I'm very fearful for the future.

00:51:20.830 --> 00:51:23.000
- Yeah, well Randi thank you so much.

00:51:23.000 --> 00:51:25.575
I mean this was an
incredible conversation.

00:51:25.575 --> 00:51:27.820
As I said, I could keep going.

00:51:27.820 --> 00:51:29.865
But I know in this time

00:51:29.865 --> 00:51:32.450
to have any time with you is a real honor

00:51:32.450 --> 00:51:33.900
and I think you've really helped

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:35.740
a lot of the folks watching

00:51:35.740 --> 00:51:37.720
understand all of the issues
that play a lot better,

00:51:37.720 --> 00:51:39.200
so thank you so much.

00:51:39.200 --> 00:51:41.100
- Thank you, thank you for everything.

00:51:43.430 --> 00:51:46.100
- So thanks everyone for
joining today's conversation.

00:51:46.100 --> 00:51:50.190
Obviously we went over,
my apologies to Randi,

00:51:50.190 --> 00:51:53.480
I probably kept her longer
than we originally promised,

00:51:53.480 --> 00:51:57.090
but I thought there was
just too much to talk about.

00:51:57.090 --> 00:51:59.693
Frankly there's more
(laughing) I would like to.

00:51:59.693 --> 00:52:03.720
But I got a couple of very
powerful takeaways there.

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:07.563
Obviously safety first, we're
in the middle of a pandemic.

00:52:08.770 --> 00:52:12.130
What I heard is, what I
thought made a lot of sense is,

00:52:12.130 --> 00:52:13.780
when you can create safe environments,

00:52:13.780 --> 00:52:15.810
and let's be creative about
creating safe environments,

00:52:15.810 --> 00:52:19.730
with ventilation with maybe
it's the younger kids,

00:52:19.730 --> 00:52:22.120
maybe it's not a full
day, whatever it might be.

00:52:22.120 --> 00:52:25.290
You meet up in the park,
those are reasonable options.

00:52:25.290 --> 00:52:27.283
But when community spread is very high,

00:52:28.220 --> 00:52:31.010
we kind of have to hunker down
as a little bit of society.

00:52:31.010 --> 00:52:33.330
And I also heard on
the instructional side,

00:52:33.330 --> 00:52:36.770
let's not try to do
everything for everyone.

00:52:36.770 --> 00:52:37.813
That'll just create a lot of stress.

00:52:37.813 --> 00:52:39.710
And it's frankly an unrealistic goal,

00:52:39.710 --> 00:52:40.934
we're in the middle of a pandemic.

00:52:40.934 --> 00:52:42.790
Try to focus on the basics

00:52:42.790 --> 00:52:44.735
and the most basic of basics

00:52:44.735 --> 00:52:46.810
is kind of the Maslow's hierarchy,

00:52:46.810 --> 00:52:51.570
people feeling safe and secure
and part of a community.

00:52:51.570 --> 00:52:53.870
And if they have that, if
their mental wellbeing is good,

00:52:53.870 --> 00:52:55.290
their social wellbeing is good,

00:52:55.290 --> 00:52:57.040
then we can start to build up on that.

00:52:57.040 --> 00:52:58.600
And once again we don't
have to do everything.

00:52:58.600 --> 00:52:59.639
But if we can get some
of those core skills

00:52:59.639 --> 00:53:02.350
of math, reading and writing,
make sure they don't atrophy,

00:53:02.350 --> 00:53:03.810
make sure we have some progression,

00:53:03.810 --> 00:53:07.527
that we're gonna be holding
the fort reasonably well

00:53:07.527 --> 00:53:09.410
when it comes to education.

00:53:09.410 --> 00:53:11.051
So thanks everyone for joining

00:53:11.051 --> 00:53:13.430
and thanks for your great questions.

00:53:13.430 --> 00:53:16.830
I will see you, I'm
actually going on a mini,

00:53:16.830 --> 00:53:18.660
a much needed camping trip tomorrow.

00:53:18.660 --> 00:53:20.543
But I will see you next week.

